    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/08/nike-vomero-plus-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning is slight-to-moderately soft (not mushy) with strong impact protection from the 43/33 mm full-length ZoomX stack; heel transitions are smooth from the posterolateral bevel, and the forefoot feels rockered with limited flex.",
        "Responsiveness is a major strength: ZoomX provides high bounce and energy return for daily running and moderate uptempo work, but it feels less snappy when pushed faster than tempo pace.",
        "Stability is neutral but improved over prior Invincible models: large heel sidewalls, a stiff heel counter, and mild lateral guidance add support, though the tall soft platform can still feel a bit unstable on longer efforts.",
        "Durability appears above average: at about 100 miles there was only mild outsole lug wear with the outsole intact, and the midsole largely preserved its bounce while becoming slightly firmer."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/07/nike-vomero-plus-initial-review-big-fun.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The 45 mm heel / 35 mm forefoot all-ZoomX setup is described as very plush with strong impact protection and a deep, protective heel/forefoot feel, though one tester found it a bit firmer than the Vomero 18.",
        "Responsiveness: Testers repeatedly call the deep ZoomX midsole energetic and bouncy with better response than the Vomero 18, but one reviewer said turnover can feel lazy/long instead of very snappy.",
        "Stability: Stability is considered good for such a soft max-stack shoe due to deep foot sit in sidewalls, a stout heel counter, wider midfoot platform, and full-coverage waffle outsole, though some reported forefoot lockdown/roominess issues.",
        "Durability: Reviewers broadly report durability as excellent, citing the fuller, more substantial waffle outsole and reinforced upper/toe bumper construction."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/nike-vomero-plus-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Max-cush 45/35 mm full-length ZoomX delivers a very plush, squishy ride with strong impact protection and a soft heel-to-forefoot feel.",
        "Responsiveness: The ride is described as bouncy and energetic with strong perceived energy return, feeling more responsive than the Vomero 18 despite using TPE-based (not PEBA) ZoomX.",
        "Stability: It appears less stable than the firmer Vomero 18; the review gives no specific torsional rigidity, heel-counter stiffness, or lateral support measurements.",
        "Durability: No meaningful outsole wear or material longevity data is provided, so durability cannot be reliably assessed from this review."
      ]
    }
